CamelCase compound preservation in BM25 tokenization — exact identifier matches outrank partial via IDF

### Symptom

BM25 keyword search over code-domain text fails to distinguish exact identifier matches from coincidental word matches. Searching for `ChatModel` ranks documents containing unrelated "chat" and "model" equally to documents about the `ChatModel` Java interface.

### Context

Standard BM25 tokenizers (word-boundary split + lowercase) produce `chat` and `model` from `ChatModel`. A document about "a chat room model" matches identically to one about `dev.langchain4j.model.chat.ChatModel`. The compound identifier is destroyed.

### Fix

Produce both the compound form AND the components during tokenization:

```
ChatModel → [chatmodel, chat, model]
DefaultBean → [defaultbean, default, bean]
HTTPClient → [httpclient, http, client]
```

The compound token `chatmodel` is rare (high IDF) — it appears only in documents that actually discuss `ChatModel`. The component tokens `chat` and `model` are common (low IDF). BM25's IDF weighting naturally ranks documents matching the rare compound higher than documents matching only the common components.

```java
static List<String> tokenize(String text) {
    List<String> tokens = new ArrayList<>();
    String[] segments = text.split("[^a-zA-Z0-9]+");
    for (String segment : segments) {
        if (segment.isEmpty()) continue;
        List<String> parts = splitCamelCase(segment);
        for (String part : parts) tokens.add(part.toLowerCase());
        if (parts.size() > 1) tokens.add(segment.toLowerCase());
    }
    return tokens;
}
```

### Why this is non-obvious

The standard approach to CamelCase tokenization is to split into components only — `ChatModel → [chat, model]`. Keeping the compound alongside the components seems redundant until you realize IDF does the ranking work: the compound is always rarer than its components, so BM25 automatically favors exact matches without any special scoring logic. The insight is that the tokenizer controls ranking quality through IDF statistics, not through scoring formulas.

QuarkusTestProfile.getEnabledAlternatives() replaces quarkus.arc.selected-alternatives — does not merge

## Symptom

A test using `@TestProfile` with `getEnabledAlternatives()` returning `Set.of(MyTestBean.class)` causes `@Alternative` beans selected via `quarkus.arc.selected-alternatives` in `application.properties` to be silently deactivated. For example, `MemoryPlanItemStore` stops being active and the application starts with `JpaPlanItemStore` instead — typically causing a startup failure (`HumanTaskRecoveryService` queries `WorkAdapterPlanItemEntity` which isn't mapped to Hibernate in the test context) or a wrong bean injection with no diagnostic pointing at the profile.

## Root Cause

`getEnabledAlternatives()` in `QuarkusTestProfile` **replaces** the effective set of selected alternatives for that profile — it does not ADD to the `quarkus.arc.selected-alternatives` value in `application.properties`. Any `@Alternative` bean that relies on the config property for activation is deactivated if not explicitly listed in `getEnabledAlternatives()`.

## Fix

**Option A:** Include ALL required `@Alternative` beans in `getEnabledAlternatives()` — not just the test-specific one:

```java
@Override
public Set<Class<?>> getEnabledAlternatives() {
    return Set.of(
        MyTestBean.class,
        MemoryPlanItemStore.class,
        MemorySubCaseGroupRepository.class,
        JpaLedgerEntryRepository.class
        // every @Alternative from application.properties goes here
    );
}
```

**Option B:** Replace the `@TestProfile` + `@Alternative` inner class with `@InjectMock` on the SPI interface. This avoids the profile entirely and leaves the standard `selected-alternatives` config intact:

```java
@QuarkusTest
class MyTest {
    @InjectMock MySpi spi;

@BeforeEach
    void setup() {
        when(spi.evaluate(any())).thenReturn(testResult);
    }
}
```

## Why This Is Non-Obvious

The name `getEnabledAlternatives()` implies additive behaviour — "enable these alternatives (in addition to the defaults)". The actual semantics are replacement — enabling these alternatives IS the complete set. The `application.properties` config is ignored in favour of the profile's set, with no log message indicating that previously-selected alternatives have been dropped.

