JPA test fixtures break silently when API migrates from direct object pass to store lookup

## Symptom

After migrating an API from accepting an in-memory entity object to accepting an ID and doing a store lookup internally, existing tests fail with wrong-value assertions — not compilation errors. The test appears to set up the entity correctly, but the asserted fields have default/null values instead of the test-specific ones.

Example: 5 tests in `WorkItemTemplateServiceResolutionTest` failed after migrating from `templateService.instantiate(template, ...)` to `templateService.createFromTemplate(request)`:

```
assertThat(workItem.excludedUsers).contains("alice");
// Expected: "alice"
// Actual:   null
```

## Context

The test pattern that breaks:

```java
@Transactional
WorkItemTemplate persist(String name) {
    WorkItemTemplate t = new WorkItemTemplate();
    t.name = name;
    t.createdBy = "test";
    WorkItemTemplate.persist(t);
    return t;
}

@Test
void test() {
    WorkItemTemplate t = persist("Review");
    t.excludedGroups = "legal-team";  // set AFTER persist returns
    // Old API: templateService.instantiate(t, ...) — uses the in-memory object directly
    // New API: templateService.createFromTemplate(request) — looks up by request.templateId
}
```

The old API received the template object directly, so the in-memory modification (`t.excludedGroups = "legal-team"`) was visible. The new API does `templateStore.get(request.templateId)` which fetches a fresh entity from the database — the post-persist modification was never flushed and is invisible to the store lookup.

## Root Cause

The `persist()` helper is `@Transactional`. When the test method is NOT `@Transactional`, `persist()` commits in its own transaction boundary. The returned entity is detached. Modifications to the detached entity are in-memory only — they never reach the database. The old API masked this because it used the caller's in-memory object; the new API exposes it by doing a fresh store lookup.

## Fix

Set entity properties before persisting, not after:

```java
@Transactional
WorkItemTemplate persistWith(String name, Consumer<WorkItemTemplate> customizer) {
    WorkItemTemplate t = new WorkItemTemplate();
    t.name = name;
    t.createdBy = "test";
    customizer.accept(t);  // set properties BEFORE persist
    WorkItemTemplate.persist(t);
    return t;
}

@Test
void test() {
    WorkItemTemplate t = persistWith("Review", tmpl -> {
        tmpl.excludedGroups = "legal-team";
    });
    // Now createFromTemplate() fetches the correct values from the store
}
```

## Why This Is Non-Obvious

The test looks correct — the entity has the right properties in memory, the API call uses the entity's ID, and there are no compilation errors. The failure mode is a wrong-value assertion, not an exception. The root cause (detached entity vs fresh store lookup) requires understanding both the JPA persistence context lifecycle AND the difference between the old and new API's data source.

@Transactional on @BeforeAll static methods in @QuarkusTest has no effect — CDI interceptors cannot intercept static methods; results in ContextNotActive at runtime

## Symptom

`@BeforeAll @Transactional static void seedData()` appears to work but occasionally throws `ContextNotActive` at runtime, or the `@Transactional` annotation is silently ignored:

```
io.quarkus.arc.ArcUndeclaredThrowableException: Error invoking subclass method
    Caused by: jakarta.enterprise.context.ContextNotActiveException
```

## Context

CDI interceptors (including `@Transactional`) use proxy subclassing to intercept method calls. Static methods cannot be overridden by a subclass — CDI proxies have no mechanism to intercept them. The annotation is present but has no effect.

Panache active record operations (`Entity.find(...)`, `entity.persist()`) require an active transaction. Without CDI-managed transaction boundaries, they fail when called from a static context.

## Root cause

CDI 3.0 (Jakarta EE 9) spec §7.2: "An interceptor is not invoked for a private, static, or final method." Static methods are explicitly excluded from CDI interception.

## Fix

Convert static seeding methods to instance methods using `@TestInstance(PER_CLASS)` or `@BeforeEach`:

```java
// Wrong — @Transactional is silently ignored:
@BeforeAll
@Transactional
static void seedData() {
    MyEntity entity = new MyEntity();
    entity.persist();  // fails — no active transaction
}

// Correct — @BeforeEach instance method, CDI intercepted:
@BeforeEach
@Transactional
void seedData() {
    if (MyEntity.count() == 0) {
        MyEntity entity = new MyEntity();
        entity.persist();
    }
}
```

Or use `@TestInstance(TestInstance.Lifecycle.PER_CLASS)` to allow `@BeforeAll` on instance methods:

```java
@QuarkusTest
@TestInstance(TestInstance.Lifecycle.PER_CLASS)
class MyTest {
    @BeforeAll
    @Transactional
    void seedData() {  // instance method — CDI interception works
        ...
    }
}
```

## Why this is non-obvious

The annotation compiles, the test runs, and if Panache's autocommit behaviour happens to work for the specific operation, tests may pass intermittently or in certain environments. The failure only surfaces when the transaction boundary is actually required (e.g. Panache `merge()`, FK constraints). The spec exclusion is documented but not surfaced as a compiler warning.

**See also:** [GE-20260512-66d997](GE-20260512-66d997.md) — Panache static methods bypass CDI @Alternative stores

CDI @Alternative reactive wrapper with private new Impl() delegate is a separate instance from the CDI bean — @AfterEach clear() silently targets the wrong object

### Symptom

`@AfterEach` calls `channelStore.clear()` on an injected CDI bean but state leaks between tests — subsequent tests see data from previous test runs despite the cleanup.

### Context

A Quarkus test class has:
```java
@Inject InMemoryChannelStore channelStore;   // CDI @Alternative @Priority(1)

@AfterEach void cleanUp() { channelStore.clear(); }
```
A reactive wrapper bean also exists:
```java
@Alternative @Priority(1) @ApplicationScoped
public class InMemoryReactiveChannelStore implements ReactiveChannelStore {
    private final InMemoryChannelStore delegate = new InMemoryChannelStore();
    ...
}
```

### Root cause

`InMemoryReactiveChannelStore` creates its delegate via `new InMemoryChannelStore()` — plain Java instantiation, not CDI injection (`@Inject`). The CDI container manages:
- One `InMemoryChannelStore` bean (injected into the test)
- One `InMemoryReactiveChannelStore` bean (used by reactive services)

The reactive wrapper's `delegate` field is a **third, separate object** — not the CDI bean. Services injecting `ReactiveChannelStore` write to the wrapper's private delegate. `clear()` on the CDI `InMemoryChannelStore` bean clears the wrong instance.

### Fix

Inject the type that the actual service uses:
```java
// If service uses blockingChannelService → ChannelStore:
@Inject InMemoryChannelStore channelStore;        // correct for blocking path

// If service uses ReactiveChannelStore:
@Inject InMemoryReactiveChannelStore channelStore; // has its own clear()
```

Verify which path the service under test takes by reading its source — field type determines which CDI bean it receives.

### Why this is non-obvious

Both `InMemoryChannelStore` and `InMemoryReactiveChannelStore` are `@ApplicationScoped @Alternative @Priority(1)` CDI beans — they appear symmetric. The internal `new InMemoryChannelStore()` delegation is an implementation detail only visible in source code. `clear()` returns void and throws no error, so state leakage is the only signal — and that's often masked by test ordering or unique identifiers.

@Alternative on a CDI bean silently suppresses all @DefaultBean beans of the same type — they vanish from the container entirely

### Symptom

`Instance<ChatModel>` iteration returns only the `@Alternative` bean. The `@DefaultBean` ChatModel (registered by quarkus-langchain4j via `SyntheticBeanBuildItem.defaultBean()`) is completely absent from the container — not deprioritized, gone.

### Context

A platform module needed to provide a `ChatModel` adapter (`@Alternative @Priority(10)`) that wraps an `AgentProvider` as a `ChatModel`. The same deployment also has quarkus-langchain4j providing a raw `ChatModel` as `@DefaultBean`. A second bean uses `Instance<ChatModel>` to find the raw model — but after filtering out the adapter, nothing remains.

### What was tried (didn't work)

`@Alternative @Priority(10) @ApplicationScoped` on the adapter bean. Expected: both the adapter and the raw `@DefaultBean` ChatModel visible in `Instance<ChatModel>`, with `@Priority` disambiguating for direct injection. Actual: the raw `@DefaultBean` ChatModel vanishes entirely.

### Root cause

ArC's `@DefaultBean` semantics state: *"the bean will only be used if no other bean of this type is configured."* An `@Alternative` with `@Priority` counts as "another bean" — it suppresses the `@DefaultBean` entirely, removing it from the container. This is not priority ordering — it is total suppression.

### Fix

Use `@DefaultBean @Priority(10)` instead of `@Alternative @Priority(10)`.

```java
// WRONG — suppresses quarkus-langchain4j's @DefaultBean ChatModel
@Alternative
@Priority(10)
@ApplicationScoped
public class AgentProviderChatModel implements ChatModel { ... }

// RIGHT — coexists with other @DefaultBean ChatModel beans
@DefaultBean
@Priority(10)
@ApplicationScoped
public class AgentProviderChatModel implements ChatModel { ... }
```

Two `@DefaultBean` beans coexist — neither suppresses the other. `@Priority(10)` wins for direct `@Inject ChatModel` injection. `Instance<ChatModel>` sees both, enabling runtime filtering.

A consumer-provided non-`@DefaultBean` ChatModel properly overrides both defaults — the `@DefaultBean` suppression rule works as expected when a "real" bean is present.

### Why this is non-obvious

Developers expect `@Alternative` to win the injection point but leave other beans in the container for `Instance` lookup. The mental model is "priority ordering among all candidates" when the reality is "total removal of @DefaultBean beans when any non-default bean exists."

The ArC javadoc confirms: *"Default beans can optionally declare @Priority. Priority value is used for bean ordering and during typesafe resolution to disambiguate multiple matching default beans."* This `@DefaultBean @Priority` pattern is the correct way to have two platform-provided defaults with deterministic selection.

### Why this fix

`@DefaultBean @Priority` is architecturally correct — both beans are "defaults" that yield to consumer-provided beans. `@Alternative` implies the bean is an override, which is semantically wrong when both beans are platform infrastructure that should coexist.

### Alternatives considered

- **CDI qualifiers** (`@Named`, custom qualifier) — would work but forces callers to qualify injection points. Breaks the "just add to classpath" activation model.
- **Conditional activation** (`@IfBuildProperty`) — too rigid; the coexistence scenario is legitimate in deployments using both adapters.

**See also:** GE-20260601-fcf0d9 — two @DefaultBean beans for the same type give AmbiguousResolutionException, not Unsatisfied | GE-20260623-c651a1 — removing @Alternative from one bean in a multi-implementation chain silently changes resolution

Non-OIDC SecurityIdentity triggers MissingTenancyException from OidcCurrentPrincipal — production-only regression invisible to tests

### Symptom

In production, endpoints that call `CurrentPrincipal.tenancyId()` via JPA store queries throw `MissingTenancyException` → HTTP 500 when the request was authenticated by a custom (non-OIDC) `HttpAuthenticationMechanism`. Tests always pass — the regression is invisible to the entire test suite.

### Context

A custom auth mechanism creates a non-anonymous `SecurityIdentity` (with roles, attributes, but no JWT) for service-to-service calls. `OidcCurrentPrincipal @Alternative @Priority(100)` is the winning `CurrentPrincipal` in production. JPA stores inject `CurrentPrincipal` and call `tenancyId()` in every query.

### What was tried (didn't work)

1. Using `@CrossTenant` stores — `CrossTenantCommitmentStore` lacks `findByCorrelationId()`, can't cover all endpoint operations
2. Setting `SecurityIdentity.anonymous=true` with roles — `policy=authenticated` rejects anonymous identities, and anonymous+roles is semantically wrong

### Root cause

`OidcCurrentPrincipal.tenancyId()` assumes non-anonymous identities always have a JWT:

```java
return identity.isAnonymous()
    ? DEFAULT_UUID
    : jwt.claim("tenancyId").orElseThrow(MissingTenancyException::new);
```

A custom mechanism creates a non-anonymous SecurityIdentity without JWT data. `isAnonymous()` returns false → falls through to `jwt.claim()` → no JWT → `MissingTenancyException`.

Tests never catch this because `FixedCurrentPrincipal @Alternative @Priority(200)` wins in tests, masking the `OidcCurrentPrincipal` code path entirely.

### Fix

App-local `CurrentPrincipal @Alternative @Priority(150)` that detects bridge-authenticated identities via a SecurityIdentity attribute and returns the default tenancyId:

```java
@RequestScoped
@Alternative
@Priority(150) // between OidcCurrentPrincipal(100) and FixedCurrentPrincipal(200)
public class AppCurrentPrincipal implements CurrentPrincipal {
    @Inject SecurityIdentity identity;
    @Inject OidcCurrentPrincipal oidcPrincipal; // by concrete type — no alternative competition

private static final String DEFAULT_TENANCY = "278776f9-e1b0-46fb-9032-8bddebdcf9ce";

@Override
    public String tenancyId() {
        Boolean bridge = identity.getAttribute("casehub.plugin.bridge");
        if (bridge != null && bridge) return DEFAULT_TENANCY;
        return oidcPrincipal.tenancyId();
    }
    // delegate other methods similarly
}
```

The mechanism stamps `casehub.plugin.bridge=true` on the SecurityIdentity. The app-local CurrentPrincipal detects this attribute and returns the default tenancyId without touching the JWT path.

### Why this is non-obvious

The regression is **production-only** — no test configuration can surface it because `FixedCurrentPrincipal @Priority(200)` always wins over `OidcCurrentPrincipal @Priority(100)` in tests. The only way to discover it is bytecode analysis of `OidcCurrentPrincipal.tenancyId()` combined with tracing through `JpaCommitmentStore` query methods that call `currentPrincipal.tenancyId()` in WHERE clauses. The `save()` method does NOT call `tenancyId()` — only query methods do — so some endpoints work (commit) while others fail (done, commitments).

### Why this fix

An app-local `@Priority(150)` CurrentPrincipal is the correct adapter for an app with two auth mechanisms (OIDC + bridge). It's not a workaround — it's a proper composition of identity contexts. The platform-level fix (platform#121: OidcCurrentPrincipal checks SecurityIdentity attributes) is the long-term solution; this adapter is removable when it ships.

CDI delegate pattern: reactive in-memory store wraps blocking store to share state across both interfaces in @QuarkusTest

## Problem solved

In `@QuarkusTest`, test setup uses `@BeforeEach channelStore.put(channel)` (blocking in-memory store) because reactive alternatives like `ReactiveChannelService.create()` require a Vert.x context unavailable on JUnit test threads. But reactive service code under test injects `ReactiveChannelStore` — a different interface backed by a different implementation. The blocking `put()` is invisible to the reactive service.

## Technique

Create a separate reactive delegate bean that injects the blocking in-memory store and wraps every method in `Uni.createFrom().item()`. Because both beans are CDI singletons, they share the same backing state:

```java
@Alternative
@Priority(1)
@ApplicationScoped
public class InMemoryReactiveChannelStore implements ReactiveChannelStore {

@Inject
    InMemoryChannelStore blocking; // CDI injects the @ApplicationScoped singleton

@Override
    public Uni<Channel> put(Channel channel) {
        return Uni.createFrom().item(() -> blocking.put(channel));
    }

@Override
    public Uni<Optional<Channel>> findByName(String name) {
        return Uni.createFrom().item(() -> blocking.findByName(name));
    }

@Override
    public Uni<List<Channel>> scan(ChannelQuery query) {
        return Uni.createFrom().item(() -> blocking.scan(query));
    }

@Override
    public Uni<Void> delete(UUID id) {
        return Uni.createFrom().voidItem().invoke(ignored -> blocking.delete(id));
    }
}
```

**Why this works:**
- `@BeforeEach channelStore.put(channel)` writes to `InMemoryChannelStore`
- CDI injects the same `InMemoryChannelStore` singleton into `InMemoryReactiveChannelStore.blocking`
- Reactive service code calls `ReactiveChannelStore.findByName()` → `InMemoryReactiveChannelStore` → delegates to `InMemoryChannelStore` → sees the test data
- `@AfterEach channelStore.clear()` clears `InMemoryChannelStore`, which automatically clears what the reactive view sees

**Key:** Make the `blocking` field package-private (no `private`) so `@BeforeEach` setup code in the same package can inject a specific instance for unit tests that don't use CDI:

```java
@Inject
InMemoryChannelStore blocking = new InMemoryChannelStore(); // CDI overwrites this
```

## Why this is non-obvious

The obvious approaches both fail: you can't make one class implement both interfaces (irreconcilable return types), and you can't call `ReactiveChannelService.create()` from `@BeforeEach` (needs Vert.x context). The delegate pattern via CDI injection to share a singleton is the non-obvious third option — it resolves the shared-state problem without touching the test thread's context requirements.

**See also:** [GE-20260521-d72294](GE-20260521-d72294.md) — blocking and reactive store interfaces have irreconcilable put() signatures — explains why delegate pattern is needed | [GE-20260519-f0967f](GE-20260519-f0967f.md) — simpler @DefaultBean shim alternative

**See also:** [GE-20260518-bee1b3](GE-20260518-bee1b3.md) — virtual-thread offload + CDI self-injection: pattern for safe blocking JPA in reactive pipeline

