The library tests configure a default datasource and pass. Consumer integration fails.

## Root cause

`Panache.withTransaction(Supplier<Uni<T>>)` (zero-argument form) always uses the **default** persistence unit. When a consumer app has no default PU, Panache cannot acquire a session and throws at runtime. The library's own tests define a default datasource (usually H2 in test config), so the problem is invisible until a consumer app is built.

## Fix

Use the named-PU overload `Panache.withTransaction(String persistenceUnitName, Supplier<Uni<T>>)`:

```java
// Before — uses default PU, breaks in consumers with named-PU-only config
return Panache.withTransaction(() -> {
    return messageStore.put(message);
});

// After — explicitly targets the named PU
return Panache.withTransaction("qhorus", () -> {
    return messageStore.put(message);
});
```

Apply consistently to all transactional methods in the library — if one method uses the named form and another uses the default form, intermittent failures result depending on which code path the consumer exercises first.

## Why this is non-obvious

The zero-argument `withTransaction()` is the documented primary form. The named-PU overload is not prominently documented. The failure only appears in consumers that don't configure a default datasource — the library's own tests pass, giving false confidence. The `IllegalStateException` message ("No current Mutiny.Session") doesn't mention the PU name mismatch.

**See also:** [GE-20260512-ea776c](GE-20260512-ea776c.md) — Quarkus named persistence units silently skip schema generation

**See also:** [GE-20260530-da427e](GE-20260530-da427e.md) — Quarkus multi-PU sub-package matching assigns LedgerEntry subclass entities to wrong PU

**See also:** [GE-20260612-8e925b](GE-20260612-8e925b.md) — Quarkus quarkus.hibernate-orm.<pu-name>.sql-load-script for named PU

**See also:** [GE-20260618-08cb96](GE-20260618-08cb96.md) — Named Hibernate ORM PU requires both @PersistenceUnit qualifier AND packages config

Quarkus named persistence unit EntityManager injection uses io.quarkus.hibernate.orm.PersistenceUnit — not jakarta.persistence.PersistenceUnit

## What exists but isn't documented

When using multiple Hibernate ORM persistence units in Quarkus, injecting a specific PU's `EntityManager` requires a Quarkus-specific CDI qualifier — **not** the standard JPA annotation.

**Wrong** (standard JPA — doesn't work as a CDI qualifier):
```java
@PersistenceContext(unitName = "qhorus")
EntityManager em;
```

**Wrong** (another natural attempt):
```java
@Inject
@Named("qhorus")
EntityManager em;
```

**Correct** (Quarkus CDI qualifier):
```java
import io.quarkus.hibernate.orm.PersistenceUnit;

@Inject
@PersistenceUnit("qhorus")
EntityManager em;
```

## Context

Applies to any class that directly injects `EntityManager` when multiple Hibernate ORM persistence units are configured via `quarkus.hibernate-orm.<name>.*`. Panache-based stores (`PanacheEntityBase`, `PanacheRepositoryBase`) don't need this — they automatically route to the correct PU based on the `quarkus.hibernate-orm.<name>.packages` config covering the entity's package.

## Why non-obvious

The JPA specification defines `@PersistenceContext`/`@PersistenceUnit` for named PU injection. Quarkus replaces this mechanism with its own CDI qualifier `io.quarkus.hibernate.orm.PersistenceUnit`. The Quarkus multiple datasources documentation mentions named PUs but doesn't clearly state that CDI-style `@Inject + @PersistenceUnit` is the required pattern rather than the standard JPA annotations.

**See also:** GE-20260423-885412 — @PersistenceUnit is a standalone qualifier; adding @Inject breaks CDI resolution | GE-20260422-f86f42 — dual-PU setup when a library injects @Default EntityManager

**See also:** [GE-20260424-a29f1c](GE-20260424-a29f1c.md) — IntelliJ strips exactly this @PersistenceUnit qualifier silently; [GE-20260424-6b88a0](GE-20260424-6b88a0.md) — quarkus.ledger.datasource routes to named PU

**See also:** [GE-20260512-4d6f48](GE-20260512-4d6f48.md) — Panache entities cannot be scanned by two Quarkus persistence units simultaneously

WorkItemService.delegate() must run strategy BEFORE clearing assigneeId or Hibernate auto-flush corrupts workload counts

## Symptom

A `WorkerSelectionStrategy` that counts active WorkItems per candidate (`countActive()`)
returns 0 for the delegating actor even though they have active items. This causes
least-loaded routing to incorrectly include the delegator as a candidate with low load.

## Context

In `WorkItemService.delegate()`, the flow is:
1. Retrieve WorkItem from store (Hibernate first-level cache)
2. Set `item.assigneeId = toAssigneeId` and `item.status = PENDING`
3. Call `assignmentService.assign(item, DELEGATED)` ← workload counted here
4. Call `workItemStore.put(item)` ← Hibernate flush

The strategy's `countActive()` queries the DB for `WHERE assigneeId = :delegator AND status IN (ASSIGNED, IN_PROGRESS, SUSPENDED)`.

## What was tried

Placing the strategy call AFTER `workItemStore.put()` — Hibernate flushes on `put()`,
updating `assigneeId` to null/toAssigneeId in the DB before the query runs. The delegator
appears to have zero active items.

## Root cause

Hibernate flushes dirty entities when `persistAndFlush()` is called (inside `put()`).
If `item.assigneeId` was already cleared before the strategy reads workload counts,
the DB reflects the new state — the delegator's item no longer appears as "assigned to them".
The strategy sees an inaccurate snapshot.

## Fix

Call the assignment strategy BEFORE mutating `item.assigneeId` or `item.status`:

```java
public WorkItem delegate(UUID id, String actorId, String toAssigneeId) {
    final WorkItem item = requireWorkItem(id);
    // ... validate state ...

// CORRECT: strategy fires first, with delegator still showing their full workload
    assignmentService.assign(item, AssignmentTrigger.DELEGATED);

// Then mutate state (strategy may have already set assigneeId)
    if (item.assigneeId == null || item.assigneeId.equals(actorId)) {
        item.assigneeId = toAssigneeId;
        item.status = WorkItemStatus.PENDING;
    }
    item.delegationChain = ...;
    item.delegationState = DelegationState.PENDING;

final WorkItem saved = workItemStore.put(item);
    // Audit uses saved.assigneeId (actual result, not toAssigneeId param)
    audit(saved.id, "DELEGATED", actorId, "to:" + saved.assigneeId);
    ...
}
```

Also: use `saved.assigneeId` (not `toAssigneeId`) in the audit record — when the
strategy overrides the requested target, the audit must reflect who actually got the item.

## Why this is non-obvious

The bug is invisible unless the delegating actor has active items AND the test checks
routing outcomes after delegation. Standard Hibernate flush-on-put semantics are
well understood, but the interaction with a query-in-the-same-transaction strategy
call is subtle. The test infrastructure creates items and delegates them in one transaction,
making the timing hard to observe without specifically testing "does the delegator's
active count affect routing?".

**See also:** GE-20260421-4a9364 — JpaWorkItemStore.scan() with assigneeId also matches candidateUsers LIKE; GE-20260415-a13ed7 — @Transactional JAX-RS method writes visible immediately within same transaction

UUID v7 needs a monotonic sequence counter for cursor pagination — random bits break same-millisecond ordering

### Symptom
Cursor pagination using `(created_at DESC, id DESC)` skips or duplicates records when multiple entries are created within the same millisecond. The cursor encodes the last-seen `(timestamp, id)` pair, but same-millisecond records have identical timestamps — ordering falls to the `id` column, where random UUID v7 bits produce non-deterministic order across queries.

### Context
UUID v7 (RFC 9562 §5.7) embeds a 48-bit Unix timestamp in milliseconds, but the remaining 74 bits (after version/variant) are random. Two UUIDs generated in the same millisecond share the same timestamp prefix but have random suffixes. When used as a keyset pagination cursor alongside a timestamp column, this means same-millisecond records appear in arbitrary order — and that order can differ between the query that produced the cursor and the query that uses it.

### What was tried (didn't work)
Standard UUID v7 generation: `Instant.now()` for the timestamp + `ThreadLocalRandom` for the remaining bits. Contract tests creating 5 notifications in a tight loop produced same-millisecond records with random ordering — `find_paginatesWithCursor` test failed intermittently.

### Root cause
The random bits in UUID v7 are designed for uniqueness, not ordering. RFC 9562 §5.7 explicitly permits but does not mandate monotonic sequences within the same timestamp tick.

### Fix
Add a thread-local 12-bit sequence counter that increments within the same millisecond and resets on millisecond change. This fills the 12 "rand_a" bits of the UUID v7 layout with a monotonic counter instead of random values.

```java
public final class UUIDv7 {
    private static final ThreadLocal<long[]> STATE = ThreadLocal.withInitial(() -> new long[]{-1, 0});

public static String generate() {
        return generate(Instant.now());
    }

static String generate(Instant now) {
        long ms = now.toEpochMilli();
        long[] state = STATE.get();
        long seq;
        if (ms == state[0]) {
            seq = (state[1] + 1) & 0xFFF; // 12-bit wrap
        } else {
            state[0] = ms;
            seq = 0;
        }
        state[1] = seq;

long msb = (ms << 16) | 0x7000L | seq;  // version 7 + sequence
        long lsb = (0x2L << 62) | (ThreadLocalRandom.current().nextLong() & 0x3FFF_FFFF_FFFF_FFFFL);
        return new UUID(msb, lsb).toString();
    }
}
```

The sequence counter ensures that UUIDs generated in the same millisecond on the same thread sort in insertion order. Cross-thread ordering within the same millisecond is not guaranteed, but is not needed — cursor pagination is per-user, and notification writes are typically serialised per user.

### Why this is non-obvious
UUID v7 is advertised as "time-ordered" — the natural assumption is that time-ordering is sufficient for pagination. It is, across millisecond boundaries. The failure only appears when multiple records are created within the same millisecond, which is common in batch operations (`storeAll`) and tight loops but rare in interactive use. The intermittent nature makes it hard to reproduce.

### Alternatives considered
- `Thread.sleep(1)` between inserts — guarantees different timestamps but adds latency to batch operations
- Use `created_at` as the sole sort key — ties are unresolvable without a secondary key
- Use a database sequence instead of UUID — loses the benefits of application-generated IDs (no DB round-trip)

Dual-constructor aggregator: CDI constructor with ManagedExecutor + package-private test constructor for plain JUnit

## The Technique

Aggregators or services that use `ManagedExecutor` for CDI-context-safe parallel execution can expose a second package-private constructor for plain JUnit testing. The test constructor accepts contributors directly, sets executor to null, and switches to sequential execution:

```java
@ApplicationScoped
public class ActorStateAggregator {

private final List<ActorStateContributor> contributors;
    private final ManagedExecutor executor;

// CDI production constructor — parallel execution with CDI context propagation
    @Inject
    public ActorStateAggregator(
            @Any Instance<ActorStateContributor> contributors,
            ManagedExecutor executor) {
        this.contributors = contributors.stream().toList();
        this.executor = executor;
    }

// Test constructor — sequential execution, no executor needed
    ActorStateAggregator(List<ActorStateContributor> contributors) {
        this.contributors = contributors;
        this.executor = null;
    }

public ActorStateResponse forActor(String actorId) {
        if (executor != null) {
            // parallel via ManagedExecutor
        } else {
            // sequential for tests
            contributors.forEach(c -> runContributor(c, actorId, accumulator));
        }
    }
}
```

Test uses lambdas as contributor stubs — no CDI bootstrap required:
```java
var agg = new ActorStateAggregator(List.of(
    (id, acc) -> acc.trustScore(0.82),   // ledger stub
    (id, acc) -> { throw new RuntimeException("timeout"); }  // work stub
));
var resp = agg.forActor("agent-x");
assertThat(resp.sources()).doesNotContain("work");
```

## Why Non-obvious

Most developers reach for `@QuarkusTest + @InjectMock` to test CDI beans, which starts the full CDI container. For testing the aggregation logic itself (which contributors are called, how failures are isolated, what the response shape looks like), a plain JUnit test with the test constructor is faster and simpler. The key insight is that the dual-constructor pattern makes `executor=null` a valid sentinel that switches the implementation to a simpler sequential path — the behaviour under test (aggregation logic) is identical.

## Constraints

The test constructor must be package-private to prevent callers from bypassing CDI in production. The `executor=null` sentinel only works when sequential execution is semantically equivalent to parallel (no ordering dependencies between contributors). This pattern is distinct from GE-20260529-c1e783 (which uses `Consumer<T>` instead of `Event<T>` for CDI event firing) — here the bypass is `ManagedExecutor` rather than CDI events.

**See also:** [GE-20260529-c1e783](GE-20260529-c1e783.md) — dual-constructor CDI pattern: @Inject delegates to package-private Consumer<T> test constructor

**See also:** [GE-20260604-4bfd2c](GE-20260604-4bfd2c.md) — adding test constructor to field-injected CDI bean removes implicit no-arg (gotcha to avoid)

**See also:** [GE-20260609-75e00d](GE-20260609-75e00d.md) — package-private test constructor + @PostConstruct null guard technique

Zero-duplication reactive test doubles: wrap InMemory*Store in a delegation shell returning Uni

## Zero-duplication reactive test doubles: wrap InMemory*Store in a delegation shell returning Uni

**ID:** GE-20260420-c1d394
**Stack:** Quarkus 3.32.2, Hibernate Reactive, Mutiny

**Problem:** A dual-stack extension (blocking + reactive) needs in-memory test implementations for both `ChannelStore` (returns `T`) and `ReactiveChannelStore` (returns `Uni<T>`). Duplicating all the in-memory state and logic doubles the maintenance surface.

### Technique
Create `InMemoryReactiveChannelStore` as a thin delegation shell — it holds an `InMemoryChannelStore` instance and wraps every method call in `Uni.createFrom().item(...)`. All state and business logic stay in the blocking in-memory store:

```java
@Alternative @Priority(1) @ApplicationScoped
public class InMemoryReactiveChannelStore implements ReactiveChannelStore {

private final InMemoryChannelStore delegate = new InMemoryChannelStore();

@Override
    public Uni<Optional<Channel>> findByName(String name) {
        return Uni.createFrom().item(() -> delegate.findByName(name));
    }

@Override
    public Uni<Channel> save(Channel channel) {
        return Uni.createFrom().item(() -> delegate.save(channel));
    }

@Override
    public void clear() { delegate.clear(); }
}
```

The `@BeforeEach clear()` call resets the single shared delegate — no separate state management needed in the reactive wrapper.

### Why non-obvious
The instinct is to create a fully separate `InMemoryReactiveChannelStore` with its own `Map` fields. The delegation approach only works cleanly because the blocking in-memory store is already stateless-beyond-its-fields — no `EntityManager` or Panache plumbing to skip. The pattern also reveals that `clear()` should be on the interface, not just the impl, so tests can reset via the reactive interface.

CDI @Alternative reactive wrapper with private new Impl() delegate is a separate instance from the CDI bean — @AfterEach clear() silently targets the wrong object

### Symptom

`@AfterEach` calls `channelStore.clear()` on an injected CDI bean but state leaks between tests — subsequent tests see data from previous test runs despite the cleanup.

### Context

A Quarkus test class has:
```java
@Inject InMemoryChannelStore channelStore;   // CDI @Alternative @Priority(1)

@AfterEach void cleanUp() { channelStore.clear(); }
```
A reactive wrapper bean also exists:
```java
@Alternative @Priority(1) @ApplicationScoped
public class InMemoryReactiveChannelStore implements ReactiveChannelStore {
    private final InMemoryChannelStore delegate = new InMemoryChannelStore();
    ...
}
```

### Root cause

`InMemoryReactiveChannelStore` creates its delegate via `new InMemoryChannelStore()` — plain Java instantiation, not CDI injection (`@Inject`). The CDI container manages:
- One `InMemoryChannelStore` bean (injected into the test)
- One `InMemoryReactiveChannelStore` bean (used by reactive services)

The reactive wrapper's `delegate` field is a **third, separate object** — not the CDI bean. Services injecting `ReactiveChannelStore` write to the wrapper's private delegate. `clear()` on the CDI `InMemoryChannelStore` bean clears the wrong instance.

### Fix

Inject the type that the actual service uses:
```java
// If service uses blockingChannelService → ChannelStore:
@Inject InMemoryChannelStore channelStore;        // correct for blocking path

// If service uses ReactiveChannelStore:
@Inject InMemoryReactiveChannelStore channelStore; // has its own clear()
```

Verify which path the service under test takes by reading its source — field type determines which CDI bean it receives.

### Why this is non-obvious

Both `InMemoryChannelStore` and `InMemoryReactiveChannelStore` are `@ApplicationScoped @Alternative @Priority(1)` CDI beans — they appear symmetric. The internal `new InMemoryChannelStore()` delegation is an implementation detail only visible in source code. `clear()` returns void and throws no error, so state leakage is the only signal — and that's often masked by test ordering or unique identifiers.

CDI delegate pattern: reactive in-memory store wraps blocking store to share state across both interfaces in @QuarkusTest

## Problem solved

In `@QuarkusTest`, test setup uses `@BeforeEach channelStore.put(channel)` (blocking in-memory store) because reactive alternatives like `ReactiveChannelService.create()` require a Vert.x context unavailable on JUnit test threads. But reactive service code under test injects `ReactiveChannelStore` — a different interface backed by a different implementation. The blocking `put()` is invisible to the reactive service.

## Technique

Create a separate reactive delegate bean that injects the blocking in-memory store and wraps every method in `Uni.createFrom().item()`. Because both beans are CDI singletons, they share the same backing state:

```java
@Alternative
@Priority(1)
@ApplicationScoped
public class InMemoryReactiveChannelStore implements ReactiveChannelStore {

@Inject
    InMemoryChannelStore blocking; // CDI injects the @ApplicationScoped singleton

@Override
    public Uni<Channel> put(Channel channel) {
        return Uni.createFrom().item(() -> blocking.put(channel));
    }

@Override
    public Uni<Optional<Channel>> findByName(String name) {
        return Uni.createFrom().item(() -> blocking.findByName(name));
    }

@Override
    public Uni<List<Channel>> scan(ChannelQuery query) {
        return Uni.createFrom().item(() -> blocking.scan(query));
    }

@Override
    public Uni<Void> delete(UUID id) {
        return Uni.createFrom().voidItem().invoke(ignored -> blocking.delete(id));
    }
}
```

**Why this works:**
- `@BeforeEach channelStore.put(channel)` writes to `InMemoryChannelStore`
- CDI injects the same `InMemoryChannelStore` singleton into `InMemoryReactiveChannelStore.blocking`
- Reactive service code calls `ReactiveChannelStore.findByName()` → `InMemoryReactiveChannelStore` → delegates to `InMemoryChannelStore` → sees the test data
- `@AfterEach channelStore.clear()` clears `InMemoryChannelStore`, which automatically clears what the reactive view sees

**Key:** Make the `blocking` field package-private (no `private`) so `@BeforeEach` setup code in the same package can inject a specific instance for unit tests that don't use CDI:

```java
@Inject
InMemoryChannelStore blocking = new InMemoryChannelStore(); // CDI overwrites this
```

## Why this is non-obvious

The obvious approaches both fail: you can't make one class implement both interfaces (irreconcilable return types), and you can't call `ReactiveChannelService.create()` from `@BeforeEach` (needs Vert.x context). The delegate pattern via CDI injection to share a singleton is the non-obvious third option — it resolves the shared-state problem without touching the test thread's context requirements.

**See also:** [GE-20260521-d72294](GE-20260521-d72294.md) — blocking and reactive store interfaces have irreconcilable put() signatures — explains why delegate pattern is needed | [GE-20260519-f0967f](GE-20260519-f0967f.md) — simpler @DefaultBean shim alternative

**See also:** [GE-20260518-bee1b3](GE-20260518-bee1b3.md) — virtual-thread offload + CDI self-injection: pattern for safe blocking JPA in reactive pipeline

casehub-qhorus MessageService methods using Panache static calls bypass InMemory store in @QuarkusTest

## Symptom

In a `@QuarkusTest` that relies on `InMemoryMessageStore` (from `casehub-qhorus-testing`), calling `messageService.findAllByCorrelationId(correlationId)` returns an empty list even though messages were dispatched to that correlationId moments before in the same test. The messages exist in the InMemory store but are invisible to this method.

## Context

`OversightGateService.fulfill()` calls `messageService.findAllByCorrelationId(gateId.toString())` to look up the original COMMAND message's Long ID (needed as `inReplyTo` for the RESPONSE dispatch). In `@QuarkusTest` with InMemory stores, this always returns empty — causing the service to log "no COMMAND message found" and fail open.

## Root Cause

`MessageService.findAllByCorrelationId()` is implemented with a Panache static call:

```java
public List<Message> findAllByCorrelationId(final String correlationId) {
    return Message.<Message> find("correlationId = ?1 ORDER BY id ASC", correlationId).list();
}
```

This bypasses the `MessageStore` interface entirely and queries Hibernate/H2 directly. The `InMemoryMessageStore` (the `@Alternative @Priority(1)` test bean) stores messages in a `ConcurrentHashMap` — it is never consulted by Panache static calls.

Most other `MessageService` methods inject and delegate to `MessageStore` — those work correctly with InMemory stores. Only the methods that use Panache static calls are affected.

## Fix

Wrap the problematic method with `@InjectSpy` in the test class and override only the bypassing method to scan the InMemory store:

```java
@InjectSpy
MessageService messageService;

@Inject
InMemoryMessageStore messageStore; // or inject the store interface directly

@BeforeEach
void mockFindAllByCorrelationId() {
    doAnswer(inv -> {
        String correlationId = inv.getArgument(0);
        return messageStore.findAll().stream()
            .filter(m -> correlationId.equals(m.correlationId))
            .sorted(Comparator.comparingLong(m -> m.id))
            .collect(Collectors.toList());
    }).when(messageService).findAllByCorrelationId(anyString());
}
```

All other `messageService` calls continue to use the real implementation; only `findAllByCorrelationId` is bridged to the InMemory store.

## Why This Is Non-Obvious

The `InMemoryMessageStore` gives the impression that all Qhorus data access is intercepted in tests. It isn't — only access that flows through the `MessageStore` interface is intercepted. Methods that call Panache directly bypass it silently: no error, just empty results. The real `InMemoryMessageStore` is injected correctly; it simply never receives the query.

**See also:** [GE-20260512-66d997](GE-20260512-66d997.md) — Panache static methods bypass CDI @Alternative stores (general pattern)

---

**Resolved in: casehub-qhorus 0.2-SNAPSHOT (2026-06-01, casehubio/qhorus#228)** — All four Panache-direct methods (`findByCorrelationId`, `findAllByCorrelationId`, `findResponseByCorrelationId`, `findDoneByCorrelationId`) now route through `messageStore.scan()`. `MessageQuery` gained `correlationId(String)` and `messageType(MessageType)` include filters; `MessageQueryJpql` and `InMemoryMessageStore.scan()` both apply them. The `@InjectSpy` workaround is no longer needed on fixed versions. The `invalidation_triggers` field predicted this fix exactly.

### Alternative — Mock MessageService + direct fanOut (ChannelBackend testing)

**Approach:** When testing a `ChannelBackend` implementation that calls `MessageService` read methods, mock `MessageService` entirely with `@InjectMock` and trigger the backend via `gateway.fanOut()` directly instead of going through `MessageService.dispatch()`.

**When to use:** Testing the `post()` logic of a `ChannelBackend` in isolation — verifying it calls the LLM, dispatches the right `MessageType`, and sets the correct `inReplyTo`. Does not test the full dispatch pipeline.

**Pros:** Avoids the store-seam issue entirely; tests the backend in isolation; fast (no full Qhorus transaction overhead).
**Cons/trade-offs:** Does not prove the backend integrates correctly with the full dispatch→fanOut→commitment lifecycle.

```java
@QuarkusTest
class MyChannelBackendTest {
    @Inject ChannelGateway gateway;
    @Inject MyChannelBackendFactory factory;

@InjectMock MessageService messageService;   // mock to control findByCorrelationId
    @InjectMock MyLlmService llm;               // mock to control LLM output

@Test
    void queryDispatchesResponse() {
        UUID channelId = UUID.randomUUID();
        String correlationId = UUID.randomUUID().toString();

// Stub the inReplyTo lookup
        Message stubQuery = new Message();
        stubQuery.id = 42L;
        when(messageService.findByCorrelationId(correlationId)).thenReturn(Optional.of(stubQuery));
        when(llm.review(any(), any(), any(), any(), any()))
            .thenReturn(new ReviewResult(false, "Looks good."));

// Register the backend
        factory.put(session);
        gateway.initChannel(channelId, new ChannelRef(channelId, "my-prefix/" + sessionId));

// Trigger via fanOut — bypasses MessageService.dispatch() entirely
        gateway.fanOut(channelId, "my-prefix/" + sessionId, new OutboundMessage(
            UUID.randomUUID(), "human:user", MessageType.QUERY, "Is this clear?",
            UUID.fromString(correlationId), null, ActorType.HUMAN));

// Verify the backend dispatched a RESPONSE
        ArgumentCaptor<MessageDispatch> captor = ArgumentCaptor.forClass(MessageDispatch.class);
        verify(messageService).dispatch(captor.capture());
        assertThat(captor.getValue().type()).isEqualTo(MessageType.RESPONSE);
        assertThat(captor.getValue().inReplyTo()).isEqualTo(42L);
    }
}
```

**See also:** [GE-20260608-5087c8](GE-20260608-5087c8.md) — @QuarkusTest @InjectSpy MessageService: COMMAND with correlationId does not trigger ChannelBackend.post()

@Alternative on a CDI bean silently suppresses all @DefaultBean beans of the same type — they vanish from the container entirely

### Symptom

`Instance<ChatModel>` iteration returns only the `@Alternative` bean. The `@DefaultBean` ChatModel (registered by quarkus-langchain4j via `SyntheticBeanBuildItem.defaultBean()`) is completely absent from the container — not deprioritized, gone.

### Context

A platform module needed to provide a `ChatModel` adapter (`@Alternative @Priority(10)`) that wraps an `AgentProvider` as a `ChatModel`. The same deployment also has quarkus-langchain4j providing a raw `ChatModel` as `@DefaultBean`. A second bean uses `Instance<ChatModel>` to find the raw model — but after filtering out the adapter, nothing remains.

### What was tried (didn't work)

`@Alternative @Priority(10) @ApplicationScoped` on the adapter bean. Expected: both the adapter and the raw `@DefaultBean` ChatModel visible in `Instance<ChatModel>`, with `@Priority` disambiguating for direct injection. Actual: the raw `@DefaultBean` ChatModel vanishes entirely.

### Root cause

ArC's `@DefaultBean` semantics state: *"the bean will only be used if no other bean of this type is configured."* An `@Alternative` with `@Priority` counts as "another bean" — it suppresses the `@DefaultBean` entirely, removing it from the container. This is not priority ordering — it is total suppression.

### Fix

Use `@DefaultBean @Priority(10)` instead of `@Alternative @Priority(10)`.

```java
// WRONG — suppresses quarkus-langchain4j's @DefaultBean ChatModel
@Alternative
@Priority(10)
@ApplicationScoped
public class AgentProviderChatModel implements ChatModel { ... }

// RIGHT — coexists with other @DefaultBean ChatModel beans
@DefaultBean
@Priority(10)
@ApplicationScoped
public class AgentProviderChatModel implements ChatModel { ... }
```

Two `@DefaultBean` beans coexist — neither suppresses the other. `@Priority(10)` wins for direct `@Inject ChatModel` injection. `Instance<ChatModel>` sees both, enabling runtime filtering.

A consumer-provided non-`@DefaultBean` ChatModel properly overrides both defaults — the `@DefaultBean` suppression rule works as expected when a "real" bean is present.

### Why this is non-obvious

Developers expect `@Alternative` to win the injection point but leave other beans in the container for `Instance` lookup. The mental model is "priority ordering among all candidates" when the reality is "total removal of @DefaultBean beans when any non-default bean exists."

The ArC javadoc confirms: *"Default beans can optionally declare @Priority. Priority value is used for bean ordering and during typesafe resolution to disambiguate multiple matching default beans."* This `@DefaultBean @Priority` pattern is the correct way to have two platform-provided defaults with deterministic selection.

### Why this fix

`@DefaultBean @Priority` is architecturally correct — both beans are "defaults" that yield to consumer-provided beans. `@Alternative` implies the bean is an override, which is semantically wrong when both beans are platform infrastructure that should coexist.

