@Path("/") root resource captures all paths in Quarkus RESTEasy Reactive — specific endpoints return 404

### Symptom

Endpoints at specific sub-paths return 404 even though the JAX-RS resource classes exist and other endpoints in the application work. Renaming the problematic resource class to use a specific `@Path` fixes the 404s.

### Context

A JAX-RS resource class uses `@Path("/")` as the class-level annotation. Method-level `@Path` annotations carry full paths (e.g. `@Path("/api/investigations/{caseId}/compliance-evidence")`). The application has other resource classes at specific paths like `@Path("/api/investigations")`.

### What was tried (didn't work)

- Verifying the resource class is CDI-discovered (`@ApplicationScoped`)
- Confirming the method-level `@Path` values are syntactically correct
- Checking that the endpoint URL matches exactly what the method-level `@Path` declares

### Root cause

`@Path("/")` makes the resource a root resource whose template matches every URI as a prefix. In RESTEasy Reactive's dispatch algorithm, `@Path("/")` is selected as a candidate for *all* requests including those with URI paths that match more-specific resource classes. When no method-level `@Path` on the root resource matches the request, RESTEasy Reactive returns 404 instead of continuing to evaluate more-specific candidates.

The JAX-RS specification requires selecting the most-specific matching resource class, but "matching" first means the class-level template must be a prefix of the request URI — and `"/"` is a prefix of everything. The tie-breaking rules then apply within the matched candidate set in ways that do not always favour more-specific classes when a root resource is in play.

### Fix

Use a fully-specified class-level `@Path` rather than `"/"`:

```java
// BAD — captures everything
@Path("/")
public class AmlLayer7Resource {
    @GET
    @Path("/api/investigations/{caseId}/compliance-evidence")
    public Response get(@PathParam("caseId") UUID caseId) { ... }
}

// GOOD — class-level path is the full endpoint path
@Path("/api/investigations/{caseId}/compliance-evidence")
public class AmlComplianceEvidenceResource {
    @GET
    public Response get(@PathParam("caseId") UUID caseId) { ... }
}
```

When two endpoints need completely different path prefixes, use two separate resource classes each with a specific class-level `@Path`:

```java
@Path("/api/investigations/{caseId}/compliance-evidence")
class AmlComplianceEvidenceResource { @GET public Response get(...) }

@Path("/api/actors/{actorId}/erasure")
class AmlGdprErasureResource { @POST public ErasureResult post(...) }
```

Package-private classes work for CDI discovery in Quarkus — Java's one-public-class-per-file rule means the second class must be package-private, which Quarkus CDI handles correctly.

### Why this is non-obvious

The JAX-RS specification describes a path selection algorithm that should favour more-specific paths. `@Path("/")` feels like a reasonable root path that method-level annotations extend. The actual 404 response looks like the endpoint URL is wrong, not like a routing conflict. The symptom only surfaces when more-specific resource classes exist in the same application.

**See also:** [GE-20260608-bde9a4](GE-20260608-bde9a4.md) — RESTEasy Reactive: return CompletionStage<Response> to chain async engine calls without @Blocking

**See also:** [GE-20260601-13fc26](GE-20260601-13fc26.md) — JAX-RS §4.2.4: IOException from message body reader bypasses all exception mappers

byte[] + EventFormatProvider.deserialize() — correct CloudEvents deserialization in JAX-RS (not auto-binding)

## Context

Implementing a JAX-RS endpoint that accepts structured CloudEvents (`Content-Type: application/cloudevents+json`) and auto-binds the body to a `CloudEvent` parameter.

## What Developers Normally Try

Declare a `CloudEvent event` parameter in the JAX-RS method and expect Quarkus REST's Jackson integration to handle deserialization:

```java
@POST
@Consumes("application/cloudevents+json")
public Response receive(CloudEvent event) { ... }  // BROKEN
```

## Why That Fails

Two compounding problems:

1. `io.cloudevents.CloudEvent` is an interface. Jackson cannot deserialize to an interface without a registered `@JsonDeserialize` annotation or a custom deserializer registered on the `ObjectMapper`.
2. Even with `cloudevents-json-jackson` on the classpath (which registers a Jackson `JsonDeserializer<CloudEvent>` via Jackson's `Module` SPI), Quarkus REST's Jackson `MessageBodyReader` is registered for `application/json`, not `application/cloudevents+json`. Quarkus REST will not pick it up for the `application/cloudevents+json` content type — the media type matching fails before deserialization is even attempted.

## The Correct Approach

Accept `byte[]` (a basic JAX-RS type that works with any content type) and deserialize manually using the CloudEvents SDK format API:

```java
@Startup
@ApplicationScoped
@Path("/webhook")
public class WebhookResource {

private EventFormat eventFormat;

@PostConstruct
    void init() {
        // cloudevents-json-jackson registers JsonFormat via ServiceLoader
        // (META-INF/services/io.cloudevents.core.format.EventFormat)
        eventFormat = EventFormatProvider.getInstance()
            .resolveFormat(JsonFormat.CONTENT_TYPE);
        if (eventFormat == null) throw new IllegalStateException(
            "cloudevents-json-jackson missing from classpath");
    }

@POST
    @Consumes("application/cloudevents+json")  // 415 for wrong Content-Type
    public Response receive(byte[] body) {
        CloudEvent event;
        try {
            event = eventFormat.deserialize(body);
        } catch (RuntimeException e) {
            return Response.status(400).entity(e.getMessage()).build();
        }
        // enrich and fire to CDI
        return Response.accepted().build();
    }
}
```

Required dependencies:
```xml
<dependency>
    <groupId>io.quarkus</groupId>
    <artifactId>quarkus-rest-jackson</artifactId>
</dependency>
<dependency>
    <groupId>io.cloudevents</groupId>
    <artifactId>cloudevents-json-jackson</artifactId>  <!-- registers JsonFormat via ServiceLoader -->
</dependency>
```

`@Consumes("application/cloudevents+json")` still works correctly for content-type enforcement (returns 415 for wrong type) — only auto-binding to a `CloudEvent` parameter fails.

`@Startup @ApplicationScoped` is required so `@PostConstruct` runs at startup (not on first request), ensuring `eventFormat` is ready and the null check fails fast on classpath misconfiguration.

## Why This Is Non-Obvious

The pattern of accepting `byte[]` and deserializing manually contradicts the normal JAX-RS expectation that the framework handles deserialization based on content type and Java type. The Jackson module distinction (Jackson `JsonDeserializer` vs JAX-RS `MessageBodyReader`) is not visible to application developers. The CloudEvents SDK's `EventFormatProvider` + ServiceLoader pattern is not mentioned in the Quarkus REST documentation.
